    "summary": "Fiery second set with epic The Other One and tight Playing In The Band",
    "blurb": "The Austin show showcases a blistering second‑set marathon, highlighted by an expansive The Other One and a crisp, swinging Playing In The Band jam. Jerry’s soaring vocals on Stella Blue and Keith’s tasteful piano add color to the high‑energy flow. Overall it ranks as one of the stronger late‑1972 performances.",
    "review": "November 22, 1972 at the Austin Municipal Auditorium finds the Dead in top form, delivering a concert that still feels fresh decades later. The first set rolls through classic rockers like Sugaree and Black Throated Wind before launching into a bright China Cat → I Know You Rider segue, but it’s the second set that truly steals the night. A seamless He’s Gone → Truckin' transition gives way to a thunderous drum break and launches into an unforgettable 12‑minute stretch of The Other One, where Phil’s thumb‑popping bass and Jerry’s white‑hot guitar intertwine with Keith’s soaring piano arpeggios. The band then glides into a beautifully sung Stella Blue before closing the set with a joyful run through Big River, Sugar Magnolia and more. Across all three soundboard sources the performance is consistently high‑energy; the Miller transfer (gd1972-11-22.sbd.fixed.miller.115765.flac16) offers crystal‑clear clarity, while the Lai capture (gd72-11-22.sbd.lai.4081.sbeok.shnf) captures the warm, roomy ambience of the house mix. Either way, listeners get a vivid snapshot of the Dead’s late‑’70s groove at one of their most inspired Texas dates.",
